{
  "name": "Democratizing AI in Africa: A New Dawn of Opportunity",
  "parent_id": 203313642253074,
  "group_id": "cd331a0e-53a6-4c43-a41f-78b10e010d55",
  "alternates": [],
  "created_at": "2026-07-31T09:52:14.317Z",
  "deleted_at": null,
  "sort_by_date": null,
  "tag_list": [],
  "updated_at": "2026-08-12T15:03:11.561Z",
  "published_at": "2026-08-12T15:03:11.547Z",
  "id": 203988723002452,
  "uuid": "d08b6a88-c16f-4f4b-822c-659d78730774",
  "is_folder": false,
  "content": {
    "_uid": "d2fe300b-55b9-9656-97a5-b34d6f7052bd",
    "body": [
      {
        "_uid": "c0c5088b-d3a1-cd3e-3039-332be7c9ce2e",
        "content": {
          "type": "doc",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "A highlight of the week was the \"AI for All\" event held on June 24 at AKU's Nairobi campus. Hosted in partnership with ",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "text": "Qhala",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "link",
                      "attrs": {
                        "href": "https://qhala.com/",
                        "uuid": null,
                        "anchor": null,
                        "custom": {},
                        "target": "_self",
                        "linktype": "url"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": ", a digital transformation firm, the event was a practical introduction to AI for non-technical professionals. Participants explored real-world applications across health, education, research, and business, and used an interactive tool to map their personal AI learning journeys. A dynamic panel discussion further showcased how AI is already improving diagnostics, teaching, business operations, and public services in Africa.",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "type": "hard_break"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Africa AI Week underscored the critical importance of AI literacy as a foundational skill for the continent's digital future. The recently launched ",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "text": "AI Talent Readiness Index for Africa",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "link",
                      "attrs": {
                        "href": "https://talentindex.ai/overall-performance",
                        "uuid": null,
                        "anchor": null,
                        "custom": {},
                        "target": "_self",
                        "linktype": "url"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": ", developed by Qhala and Qubit Hub, reveals that most African countries lack basic AI education and infrastructure. To address this, the week featured a variety of grassroots workshops, bootcamps, and community radio broadcasts, specifically targeting marginalized groups like women, refugees, and youth to bridge the existing gap. ",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "With over 60% of Africa's population under the age of 25, building AI capacity is urgent. The Index estimates the continent must train one million AI practitioners and 5,000 PhD-level researchers to remain globally competitive. Africa AI Week advocates early exposure-starting not in universities but in youth clubs, schools, and communities.",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "text": " ",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "bold"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "This movement signifies a cultural shift from passive consumption to active co-creation. Through inclusive, locally grounded programmes and strategic partnerships like AKU and Qhala, Africa is not just adapting to AI, it is shaping its trajectory. From a young girl in rural Kenya learning to code for her community to a farmer using machine learning to boost harvests, the continent's future is deeply intertwined with widespread, culturally relevant AI literacy. ",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "\"We're in the middle of an AI hype cycle-everyone's developing an app or solution,\" said Dr Fred Mutisya, Health Tech Lead at Qhala. \"But like all technology waves, this one will face a reckoning. The key is understanding where we are in the cycle. As early adopters, we must resist the temptation to abandon AI when some tools underdeliver. Instead, we need to push through the chasm, because that's where real transformation begins. Soon, AI literacy will be as essential on your CV as Word or Excel, skills like GPT or LLaMA will define the next generation of professional competence.\"",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "text": " ",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "bold"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Africa AI Week 2025 signals a bold commitment: AI is not just for experts; it's for everyone. By democratizing access and fostering local innovation, Africa is claiming its place as an author, not just a user of the AI age.",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "type": "hard_break"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "hard_break"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
